# [[ .Name ]]

A [tug](https://github.com/cuonggt/tug) app: Go handlers render [[ .Framework ]] pages
through Inertia, with no API in between. People register and verify their
email, log in, with a code from an authenticator app too if they turn that
on, or with a passkey and no password, reset a forgotten password by
email, and change their profile and photo, password and appearance in
their settings.

```sh
[[ if .SQLite -]]
tug dev      # run it on http://localhost:8080, rebuilt and reloaded as it changes
tug gen      # write the TypeScript of the pages' props and the routes
tug build    # build it into one binary, frontend included
go test ./...
[[- else -]]
docker compose up -d   # start the database, which tug dev and the tests use
tug dev                # run it on http://localhost:8080, rebuilt and reloaded as it changes
tug gen                # write the TypeScript of the pages' props and the routes
tug build              # build it into one binary, frontend included
go test ./...
[[- end ]]
```

- `main.go`: the server, its pages and routes, and the health check at
  `/up`. A page is declared with `tug.Page[Props]("Name")`, and its
  component is `resources/js/pages/Name.[[ .Component ]]`. Its pages run
  the scripts that carry their nonce alone, by a Content-Security-Policy,
  as `app.html`'s do: what it blocks, the log says.
- `auth.go`: registering, logging in and out, a forgotten password, and
  the page that asks for the password again, on tug's package `auth`. A
  route for users is wrapped in `usersOnly`, whose handler is handed the
  user, and in `verified` or `passwordConfirmed` for what needs them.
  Logging in, codes, and mail are throttled, with counts in the database
  that every instance of the app shares.
- `verify.go`: verifying an email by a mailed link. `twofactor.go`:
  two-factor logins. `passkeys.go`: passkeys, which browsers make at
  `localhost` in development, not at an IP address. `settings.go`: the
  settings pages. `photos.go`: a user's photo, which they upload, kept in
  `files/` (or `FILES_PATH`), or with `FILESYSTEM_DISK=s3` in a bucket,
  through tug's package `storage`, and shown by links signed for a day.
  `mail.go`: the mail, in plain text and HTML. `tokens.go`: API tokens,
  which a user makes in their settings, for a script or another service,
  and the API, `/api`, which takes one in place of a login, as
  `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" .../api/user`.
  `broadcasts.go`: the events a user's pages follow at `/broadcasts`, on
  their own channel, through tug's package `broadcast`, which the database
  carries to every instance of the app: the page that asks to verify the
  email moves on as the link is followed, in another tab or on a phone.
  `abilities.go`: what users may do, through tug's package `auth`, which
  a handler checks, and a no is a 403; admins may do anything. `admin.go`:
  the admins' page of the jobs that failed, `/admin/failed-jobs`, which
  runs them again, and the `admins` command, which makes someone one:
  `./[[ .Name ]] admins add ann@example.com`. `notifications.go`: what
  happened to an account, a new password or email, a passkey or a token,
  which its owner hears of by the header's bell, at once, a page of them,
  and a mail.
- `users.go`: the users. `jobs.go`: the background jobs, in the same
  database, for tug's package `queue`, which runs them beside the server.
  The mail goes by jobs, so a mail server that's down, or a restart,
  doesn't lose any: a job that fails runs again later. Jobs can run on a
  schedule too, as `prune-jobs` does every night. `QUEUE_WORKERS` is how
  many run at once.
[[- if .SQLite ]]
- `db.go`: the database, SQLite (`app.db`, or `DB_PATH`), through
  `database/sql`. The SQL of each table is beside the Go that's the same
  on any database: `users_db.go`, `passkeys_db.go`, `jobs_db.go`,
  `throttles_db.go`, `tokens_db.go`, `broadcasts_db.go`,
  `notifications_db.go`, `migrations_db.go`, and `cache_db.go`, the app's
  cache, for tug's package `cache`, which the starter keeps nothing in
  yet.
[[- else ]]
- `db.go`: the database, [[ if .MySQL ]]MySQL[[ else ]]Postgres[[ end ]] at `DB_URL`, through `database/sql`.
  `compose.yaml` runs it in development, and the tests make a database
  each on it. The SQL of each table is beside the Go that's the same on
  any database: `users_db.go`, `passkeys_db.go`, `jobs_db.go`,
  `throttles_db.go`, `tokens_db.go`, `broadcasts_db.go`,
  `notifications_db.go`, `migrations_db.go`, and `cache_db.go`, the app's
  cache, for tug's package `cache`, which the starter keeps nothing in
  yet.
[[- end ]]
- `migrations/`: the migrations that make and change the tables, a file of
  SQL each, named for when it was made, which run once, in order, as the
  app starts: `migrations.go`. `tug migrate new add_bio_to_users` writes
  the next, and `./[[ .Name ]] migrate status` lists them.
[[- if .Vue ]]
- `resources/js`: Vue with Tailwind and [shadcn-vue](https://www.shadcn-vue.com)
  components, which `npx shadcn-vue@latest add` adds more of.
[[- else if .Svelte ]]
- `resources/js`: Svelte with Tailwind and
  [shadcn-svelte](https://shadcn-svelte.com) components, in the classic
  style that shadcn/ui's have in React. Its CLI adds more of them from the
  classic registry, as its default is its newer styles:
  `COMPONENTS_REGISTRY_URL=https://shadcn-svelte.com/registry npx shadcn-svelte@latest add table`.
[[- else ]]
- `resources/js`: React with Tailwind and [shadcn/ui](https://ui.shadcn.com)
  components, which `npx shadcn@latest add` adds more of.
[[- end ]] The layouts are
  in `layouts/`, and `inertia.[[ .Script ]]`, which makes the app, says which page
  has which.
[[- if .SSR ]] `app.[[ .Script ]]` is the app in the browser, and `ssr.[[ .Script ]]`
  the app on the server: a first visit's page renders there first, with
  Node, which the app runs beside it from the SSR build in `ssr/build`,
  or under tug dev, with Vite.
[[- else ]] `app.[[ .Script ]]` is the app in the browser.
[[- end ]]
- `lang/`: the app's languages, a file each, as `tug lang vi` writes
  `lang/vi.json` with every text the app says, to translate. The binary
  embeds them, and `APP_LOCALE` is the one a browser gets that asks for
  none of them.
- `resources/js/tug`: the TypeScript tug gen writes from the Go types. It
  isn't edited by hand; tug dev and tug build write it again.
- `.env`: read by tug dev. `APP_KEY` encrypts the sessions and two-factor
  secrets and signs the links in mail and to photos, and a deployed app
  needs one of its own, set in its environment, with `APP_URL`, its own
[[- if .SQLite ]]
  address, `TRUSTED_PROXIES` behind a load balancer, and `MAIL_HOST` and
  the rest of `.env.example` for its mail, and for the photos, a volume,
  or `FILESYSTEM_DISK=s3` and a bucket. In development, tug dev sets
  `APP_URL`, and mail is written out where it shows it, links and all.
[[- else ]]
  address, `DB_URL`, its database, `TRUSTED_PROXIES` behind a load
  balancer, and `MAIL_HOST` and the rest of `.env.example` for its mail,
  and for the photos, a volume, or `FILESYSTEM_DISK=s3` and a bucket. In
  development, `DB_URL` is compose.yaml's database, tug dev sets
  `APP_URL`, and mail is written out where it shows it, links and all.
[[- end ]]
[[ if and .SSR .SQLite -]]
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image with
  Node, for the pages it renders on the server, and the database and the
  photos on a volume.
[[- else if .SSR -]]
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image with
  Node, for the pages it renders on the server, and the photos on a
  volume. The database is wherever `DB_URL` says.
[[- else if .SQLite -]]
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image, with
  the database and the photos on a volume.
[[- else -]]
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image, with
  the photos on a volume. The database is wherever `DB_URL` says.
[[- end ]]
